WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.340
Using cheat sheets when hand manipulating

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.980
stitch patterns on a manual machine is
a great way to keep track of the stitch

3
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:12.860
transfers without counting
needles or making yourself crazy.

4
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:18.220
Watch as we knit this simple lace pattern
pattern with the Knit it Now cheat sheets.

5
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.340
Based on this hand-knitting stitch

6
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.500
diagram, I reproduced the stitch
symbols on my sheet.

7
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.780
The circles are eyelets which translate
to an empty needle for machine knitting.

8
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.100
The slants represent the direction
of the stitch transfers.

9
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.020
Remember, as machine knitters,

10
00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.460
we're looking at the knitting
from the purl side.

11
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:42.700
Let's start on row one
of the stitch pattern.

12
00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:47.860
First, I'll orient the chart
to the right of the needles in work.

13
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:52.620
According to the chart,
I'll skip the first three needles and then

14
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:59.380
I'll transfer the fourth stitch left
and empty the needle, creating an eyelet.

15
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.290
Moving across the row,
I'll transfer the next eyelet left.

16
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.620
Notice that I've highlighted the eyelet
column to keep myself oriented.

17
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:16.100
Also notice that I'm working a simple
stitch transfer that will look like this.

18
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:20.050
Another option is to work
this transfer in two steps.

19
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.290
First picking up the stitch,

20
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.900
transferring it right, then picking up
both stitches to fill the emptied needle.

21
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.980
The difference is subtle and it is

22
00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:34.900
equivalent to a right leaning
decrease in hand knitting.

23
00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.700
Right leaning as seen
from the knit side of the work.

24
00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.740
I'll continue to transfer across

25
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:47.160
the row by moving and positioning
the eyelet symbols.

26
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.380
Here, I'm lining up the symbol

27
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:56.480
with the existing eyelet, positioning
the next symbol for the next transfer.

28
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.850
Shift the chart and line up the symbol

29
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.140
with the eyelet across the rows.
To double check

30
00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:08.760
you can always count the boxes
on the chart and count the needles.

31
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:10.780
Once the transfers are complete.

32
00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:14.450
My pattern is telling me to knit two rows.

33
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:16.500
But wait a minute.

34
00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.700
Oops! I didn't put the empty needles

35
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.940
into working position and they
didn't form the eyelets properly.

36
00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.480
I'll need to rip out this row and free

37
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:32.820
pass back and then put all needles
in work and continue knitting.

38
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:37.580
For the next row, I'll hold up the next
row of my chart using the orange

39
00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:43.320
highlighted column and the three
needles orientation.

40
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.340
I'll transfer according to the chart.

41
00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:03.180
One stitch on the left of the original
eyelet column and one stitch on the right.

42
00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:08.000
Again, I'm working a simple stitch
transfer on the left,

43
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.720
but this time I'm working a two
step transfer on the right.

44
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.860
This will form a left-leaning stitch.

45
00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.320
When looking at the knit side
of the knitting.

46
00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:38.100
I'll continue to work the second row
of transfers across the row using

47
00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:43.440
the orange highlighted column
to orient the chart as I go.

48
00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:52.360
This time you can see that 2-
step transfer more clearly.

49
00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.020
Remembering to put all needles in work

50
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.440
this time, I'll knit four rows,
creating the eyelets.

51
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:45.780
The next sequence of transfers are
positioned between the previous ones.

52
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.400
Line up the chart using the three stitches

53
00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.500
on the right and the orange highlighted
column to position the chart.

54
00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:57.260
Remember, the orange column
represents that first eyelet

55
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.080
on the first row.

56
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.480
Transfer left as indicated on the chart,

57
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.420
keeping the yellow highlighted
column aligned with the new eyelet.

58
00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:19.060
Transfer the next eyelet.

59
00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:25.420
Count needles if you like and use
the orange column to stay oriented.

60
00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:30.720
Slide the chart left
and finish the sequence.

61
00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.700
Notice I'm not transferring
along the edge of my piece.

62
00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:44.680
I don't want eyelets right
on the edge of my knitting.

63
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:53.440
With all needles in work, I'll knit two
rows to complete that set of eyelets.

64
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:57.540
Finish the sequence by counting needles
on the right and aligning the yellow

65
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.740
highlighted column
with the previous eyelet.

66
00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:08.560
Transfer left and right
of that center eyelet.

67
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.820
Keeping the chart aligned,

68
00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:29.600
transfer the next set of stitches
and repeat across the row.

69
00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:48.900
Keep using that yellow highlighted column

70
00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:55.600
to confirm the position of the chart
and transfer the last set of stitches.

71
00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.780
Take time to confirm the accuracy

72
00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:17.760
of the position of the eyelets
and transfers, and knit four rows.

73
00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:22.420
When looking at the knitting

74
00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:27.580
from the purl side, it's difficult to see
the right and left-leaning transfers.

75
00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.160
I suggest you experiment
with the appearance of each and use

76
00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:35.660
the appropriate technique
to get the results you want.

77
00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:41.520
Remember, a two-step transfer will lean
in the direction that the doubled-up

78
00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:45.540
stitches are transferred
in relation to the eyelet.

79
00:07:45.570 --> 00:07:49.940
For a simple stitch pattern like this,
you might want to choose to work simple

80
00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:54.980
transfers because the appearance of left
and right leaning isn't as noticeable.

81
00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:58.500
The beauty of this stitch are the eyelets,

82
00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:02.560
But be aware of the differences
with more complex stitch patterns.

